{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/651677-the-code-in-2e","id":651677,"title":"The Code in 2e","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Hunter: The Vigil"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-09-04T12:54:07-07:00","id":651677,"title":"The Code in 2e","content":"In <b>Hunter 1e</b>, hunters had Morality, a stat that was driven almost exclusively by the hunter\u2019s sense of right and wrong and focused on the consequences of his or her own actions. In that context, the Code was a means of altering one's ethics to a form that's more compatible with monster hunting \u2014 redefining the hierarchy of sins to something that\u2019s not going to call for as many degeneration checks given what the hunter does, at the cost of slowly losing touch with how ordinary people think and act. <br /> <br /> I think it would be safe to say that in <b>Hunter 2e</b>, Morality will be replaced by Integrity. I submit that, if 2e still has something akin to the Code, it will be conceptually very different from 1e\u2019s Code, in ways that mirror the differences between Morality and Integrity. Integrity still has Breaking Points based on one's ethical decisions; so you <i>can</i> continue to play the Code in that manner if you like \u2014 although what constitutes an \u201cethical Breaking Point\u201d is much more fluid under the Integrity rules, and already subject to change; so it seems to me that using the Code to address matters of personal ethics is excessive. One notable exception to this is the matter of killing: GMC makes it clear that redefining your Breaking Points so that killing someone no longer counts is not something people do. I would posit that something like the Code would be needed to change this \u2014 and that doing so would turn you into a Slasher. <br /> <br /> More importantly though, Integrity has two other kinds of Breaking Points that have very little to do with the hunter's own choices, and everything to do with what he's exposed to: you've got your violence-based Breaking Points, and you've got your supernatural-based Breaking Points. I don't have much to say about the former, other than to suggest that becoming too comfortable with violence is a good way to start encouraging someone to start becoming comfortable with <i>committing</i> violence \u2014 which in turn encourages the aforementioned step across the threshold from hunter to slasher. <br /> <br /> The latter (exposure to the supernatural) is worth noting in particular, as it has been cited by Dave Brookshaw as being part of a supernatural curse that's expressed in its fullest in <b>Mage</b> (as Quiescence, the Sleeping Curse), but which manifests in a weaker form any time a mortal encounters the supernatural. That is, this one differs from the others in that it is supernaturally imposed rather than being a mundane expression of human nature. This, I think, is where a 2e counterpart to the Code makes sense: distorting the curse in question ought to require something more than merely redefining one's Breaking Points as per the normal Integrity rules, something that tends to drive a wedge between the hunter and regular humanity. As with 1e's Code, the upside would be that encounters with (at least a class of) the supernatural cease to bother the hunter as much, and he can continue to hunt his prey without getting pulled into a downward spiral of degenerating Integrity; the downside would be that he has a harder time relating to regular humanity as he's drawn deeper into the supernatural world. For those familiar with Unknown Armies, this would be vaguely similar to that game's \u201ccallousness\u201d system. Given that encounters with the supernatural are an unavoidable part of a hunter's life, I fully expect this to be a far more important part of 2e than it ever was of 1e. <br /> <br /> Much like Integrity got renamed because it is so conceptually different from Morality (more PTSD than ethics), I suspect that the Code might also need renaming in 2e: in 1e, \u201cCode\u201d could be thought of as an abbreviation of \u201cCode of Conduct\u201d; but if its 2e counterpart is anything like what I've outlined above, I don't expect \u201cCode of Conduct\u201d to be a particularly apt description of it anymore. <br /> <br /> Thoughts?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-04T14:00:05-07:00","id":651764,"title":"","content":"Mortal Remains might be a patchjob instead of a full 2e, but I rather like the idea that it puts forth: you can still replace Breaking Points with Trigger Points like you could in 1e, which I feel is astonishingly flexible - you can replace BPs for different types of violence against monsters as well as to gain some sort of defense against the mental degeneration from some supernatural sources, with all the drawbacks that Trigger Points offer. In fact, in any sort of Hunter-centric expansion to the Integrity rules (which is probably the most we're going to see) I'd like to see the Trigger Points system revamped and redefined so it can serve much of what you're talking about while integrating fun new ideas like Conditions, Beats, etc. I feel like you could get a lot of mileage out of not a lot of wordcount with that idea.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage the Awakening 2e Communal Homebrew Threads:<br /> - <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/966394-perils-of-the-path-post-your-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">Perils of the Path</a> - Other homebrew threads linked in the OP!<br /> <br /> Join us at the unofficial IRC channels on.irc.sorcery.net at #exalted and now #theonyxpath!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/451-kirby-jerusalem","name":"Kirby Jerusalem","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar451_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"589"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-05T00:20:07-07:00","id":652192,"title":"","content":"I agree almost all the way with <b>Dataweaver</b> and <b>Kirby Jerusalem. </b>I only want to add to this...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/651677-the-code-in-2e#post651677\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This, I think, is where a 2e counterpart to the Code makes sense: distorting the curse in question ought to require something more than merely redefining one's Breaking Points as per the normal Integrity rules, something that tends to drive a wedge between the hunter and regular humanity. As with 1e's Code, the upside would be that encounters with (at least a class of) the supernatural cease to bother the hunter as much, and he can continue to hunt his prey without getting pulled into a downward spiral of degenerating Integrity; the downside would be that he has a harder time relating to regular humanity as he's drawn deeper into the supernatural world. For those familiar with Unknown Armies, this would be vaguely similar to that game's \u201ccallousness\u201d system. Given that encounters with the supernatural are an unavoidable part of a hunter's life, I fully expect this to be a far more important part of 2e than it ever was of 1e.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm playing now in <b>Hunter</b> &quot;1.5 ED&quot; game ( <b>Hunter</b> 1ED + <b>Mortal Remains</b> and <b>God Machine Rules Update</b> ) under mine buddy ST. We are under few sessions now, ending our third &quot;case&quot;. As a small combat dweller mine self, I ask him when we could use Risking Willpower mechanics as PCs. And he all the time says &quot;when you become <b><i>real</i></b> Hunters&quot; - I may think he sluggish things a bit, but from what I understand - he thinks we become &quot;full&quot; Hunters, when our psychology changes, when we are facing monsters with study conviction - you know, the very different stand than 98% of people do in World of Darkness. When you actively oppose monsters and become Candles in the Darkness.<br /> <br /> And I agree with him - you become Hunter when you change your Integrity in that way that you are not running from or forgetting supernatural, most of time. When you broke <a href=\"http://theonyxpath.com/ill-lie-to-you/\" target=\"_blank\">Lie</a> - &quot;Sleeping Curse&quot; of the World of Darkness, at least in small way - by changing your first Breaking Point for witnessing supernatural. When you dive deep in monsters world and you are addicted to it.<br /> <br /> The only real problem with this take is Hunters vs Mages scenario, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/644997-hunters-vs-mages\">shown in fully in this topic</a>. Supernal Magic of Awakened is strongest enemy of Lie and makes everyone that are not mages or Sleepwalker to forget it. So the nuCode of 2ED should works with some measures to make it play in the game were Hunter PCs could hunt Mages. <b>Dataweaver</b> had idea for <i>After Action Raport</i> Tactic that could let Hunters to piece by piece collect their memories on the fight with mage that they cannot remember. Or <i>Moral Support</i> that could raise participants Integrity stat after loss from the supernatural Breaking Point. This and a like mechanics should work with Hunters take on Integrity in 2ED.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1437,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","user":"wyrdhamster","date":". 09-05-2015, 03:22 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-09T15:01:19-07:00","id":658432,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wyrdhamster</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/651677-the-code-in-2e?p=652192#post652192\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I agree almost all the way with <b>Dataweaver</b> and <b>Kirby Jerusalem. </b>I only want to add to this...<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> I'm playing now in <b>Hunter</b> &quot;1.5 ED&quot; game ( <b>Hunter</b> 1ED + <b>Mortal Remains</b> and <b>God Machine Rules Update</b> ) under mine buddy ST. We are under few sessions now, ending our third &quot;case&quot;. As a small combat dweller mine self, I ask him when we could use Risking Willpower mechanics as PCs. And he all the time says &quot;when you become <b><i>real</i></b> Hunters&quot; - I may think he sluggish things a bit, but from what I understand - he thinks we become &quot;full&quot; Hunters, when our psychology changes, when we are facing monsters with study conviction - you know, the very different stand than 98% of people do in World of Darkness. When you actively oppose monsters and become Candles in the Darkness.<br /> <br /> And I agree with him - you become Hunter when you change your Integrity in that way that you are not running from or forgetting supernatural, most of time. When you broke <a href=\"http://theonyxpath.com/ill-lie-to-you/\" target=\"_blank\">Lie</a> - &quot;Sleeping Curse&quot; of the World of Darkness, at least in small way - by changing your first Breaking Point for witnessing supernatural. When you dive deep in monsters world and you are addicted to it.<br /> <br /> The only real problem with this take is Hunters vs Mages scenario, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/644997-hunters-vs-mages\">shown in fully in this topic</a>. Supernal Magic of Awakened is strongest enemy of Lie and makes everyone that are not mages or Sleepwalker to forget it. So the nuCode of 2ED should works with some measures to make it play in the game were Hunter PCs could hunt Mages. <b>Dataweaver</b> had idea for <i>After Action Raport</i> Tactic that could let Hunters to piece by piece collect their memories on the fight with mage that they cannot remember. Or <i>Moral Support</i> that could raise participants Integrity stat after loss from the supernatural Breaking Point. This and a like mechanics should work with Hunters take on Integrity in 2ED.</div> </div> </div> </div>I run Hunter a lot like your buddy does. When I started my first full-length campaign, it was pretty blue book until the characters made the conscious decision to adopt the Vigil (which was referred to as such in-universe). On doing so, I gave them the Conviction stat from Mirrors and access to Hunter mechanics.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3570-lnodiv","name":"lnodiv","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3570_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1607"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-09-24T10:20:25-07:00","id":676543,"title":"","content":"I'm doing much the same running a compact based game. From a game play point of view you can't have your players losing Integrity every session but Hunters are also human - so plotting and carrying out the murder of a &quot;sorcerer&quot; should have some psychological consequences to someone who has just begun their vigil. To this end I redefined the questions being asked in establishing Integrity and provided more bonus dice to actions that might be common for a Hunter game, like for instance, a bonus for protecting yourself or another. This doesn't mean that Hunters are immune to supernatural horror, far from it, but it does give them a certain amount of resistance to supernatural horror without making them &quot;other&quot; or &quot;inhuman&quot; the way Vampire's Humanity or Uratha's Harmony does.<br /> <br /> So here's the homebrew I'm working with.<br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Conviction</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">The Morality system described in the God Machine Chronicle replicates the degradation most mortals experience at witnessing the terrible truths of the World of Darkness. Hunters however have chosen to abandon their notions of the world in light of what they have experienced first hand and found a means of coming to term with it. Whether a single mother who takes up the Vigil after witnessing the theft of her son or a priest letting his own blood for the first time having accepted the hidden teachings of the Knights of Saint George both hunters have seen the world illuminated for what it is.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Conviction is how hunters cope with the World of Darkness it represents the strength of commitment to the Vigil as well as their own ability to withstand the horror of what they have chosen to stand against. As such it is as much a product of the hunters own belief as well as their overall mental well being. </span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">A character stands to lose Conviction when she experiences a breaking point. Just like with Integrity, when a character performs certain actions or endures certain experiences, he might reach a breaking point. A character who has at least a single Practical beat banked can choose to spend it to forgo the roll entirely.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">A breaking point can fall into one of the following</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">categories:</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u2022 The character performs an action that either violates his personal moral code or that is considered unacceptable</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">in society.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u2022 The character witnesses something traumatic, terrifying, or that rattles his understanding of the world.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u2022 The character is the victim of an extreme supernatural attack, whether physical, emotional, or mental.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">System</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">When a character experiences a breaking point, the player rolls Resolve + Composure with a modifier based on</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">the character\u2019s Conviction rating:</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Conviction Modifier</span> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\" style=\"border-spacing: 0px; width: 1px\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">8\u201310</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">+2</span></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">7\u20136</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">+1</span></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">5\u20134</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">0</span></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">3\u20132</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u20131</span></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">1</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u20132</span></td> </tr> </table></div> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">The Storyteller can also impose modifiers based on how heinous the breaking point is, relative to the character\u2019s experience. The chart below gives some suggestions, but again, the Storyteller and the player are encouraged to develop the character\u2019s moral framework and life experience to the point that modifiers can be customized. Modifiers are cumulative, but the total modifier from circumstances should not exceed +/\u20135 dice.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Breaking Point Modifier</span> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\" style=\"border-spacing: 0px; width: 1px\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Character is protecting himself</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">+1</span></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Character is acting in accordance with his Virtue</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">+1</span></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Character is protecting another</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">+2</span></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Character is protecting a loved one</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">+3</span></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Character is acting under supernatural influence</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">+1</span></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Character is acting in accordance with his duty</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">+1</span></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Character is acting in accordance with his Vice</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u20131</span></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Witnessing the supernatural (violent)</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u20131</span></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Witnessing an accidental death</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u20131</span></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Witnessing a murder</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u20132</span></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Committing premeditated murder </span></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u20135</span></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Killing in self-defense</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u20133</span></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Killing by accident (car wreck, e.g.)</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u20133</span></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Causing visible serious injury to a person</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u20132</span></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Torture</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u20133</span></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Enduring physical torture</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u20131</span></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Enduring a supernatural attack</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u20131</span></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Witnessing a terrible supernatural occurrence</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_td\" style=\"border-width: 1px; padding: 0px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u20131-5</span></td> </tr> </table></div> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Roll Results</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Dramatic Failure: The character\u2019s world view has been damaged, perhaps beyond repair. The character suffers from traumatic stress. Lose a dot of Conviction and choose from the following Conditions (or create a new one with Storyteller approval): Broken, Fugue, or Madness. Also, take a Beat.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Failure: The character\u2019s world view has been shaken and he probably questions his sense of self, his ability to</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">relate to people, his own moral worth, or his sanity. Lose a dot of Conviction and choose one of the following Conditions (or create a new one with Storyteller approval): Guilty, Shaken, or Spooked.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Success: The character has come through the breaking point intact. He might feel guilty or upset about what happened, but he can cope. Choose one of the following Conditions (or create a new one with Storyteller approval): Guilty, Shaken, or Spooked.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Exceptional Success: The character somehow man-ages not only to survive the breaking point, but to find meaning in it, to reaffirm his own self-worth, or to pass through fire and become tempered by it. The character takes a Beat and regains a point of Willpower.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">While Conviction is slightly more robust, at least in terms of how the <i>supernatural </i> effects a character's psychological well being (again hunters have made a conscious decision to hold a light to the darkness) the murder of a human being is always a breaking point at least until a hunter redefines his definition of human.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">The following questions help to define the character's breaking points and their relationship to the Vigil.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><i>What is the worst thing your character can imagine himself doing in pursuit of the Vigil? </i></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">In choosing to confront the inhuman how inhumane is a character prepared to act? What is the line that your character would not cross? What is he prepared to do to save himself or another? Would he torture a monster's servant or use an innocent as bait?</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><i>What is the worst thing your character can imagine someone else doing?</i></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Monsters (human or otherwise) are capable of atrocities that can destroy minds and rend souls. What is the worst thing your character can imagine befalling someone (or himself)? Is it the thought of a monster destroying his family? The possession of an innocent? Cannibalism?</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><i>What can't your character forget?</i></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">A character who carries the Vigil their first exposure to the supernatural often leaves them with memories they cannot let go of. Decide what memories the character carries with them. Did she lose her father to living shadows? Watch helplessly as her mentor was burned alive by a spirit? Kill someone under a vampire's command? Describe this event in as much detail as you can. This is a breaking point in its own right and sets a benchmark for what your character would have to be exposed to experience one now.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><i>What does your character believe?</i></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">This is a belief that the character holds most deeply, the center by which a hunter holds himself in the face of experiences that threaten to overwhelm him. Note that this is something the character clings to without rational proof. Is it faith in a higher power or some conspiracy theory? Intrinsic loyalty of family? Scientific rationalism? Fate or some greater destiny?</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><i>Who or what is the enemy?</i></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">What is a monster? Where is the line between human and inhuman? Is it biological? The existence of a soul? The definition of a behavior? Or is anything with abnormal &quot;power&quot; a potential target? This establishes not just a single breaking point but helps define what the character defines as murder.</span><br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">The Code</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">When a hunter faces the choice between pursuing the hunt and taking an action that is a breaking point or abandoning the hunt to avoid the action. When the hunter chooses the hunt over their own Conviction they've come to a Trigger Point, and the player may choose to change the hunter's Code replacing one normal breaking point with one more appropriate to the hunter's obsession.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">External Costs</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Each deviation is represented by a cumulative -1 Social penalty (to a maximum of -5) when dealing with other mortals. This does not affect interactions with other hunters, Allies, Retainers, or a True Friend.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Internal Costs</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Scars</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Scars are Persistent Conditions brought about by the character's obsession with the Vigil. Unlike other Persistent Conditions there is no resolution for Scars, they are permanent psychological shifts from which there is no return. Unless otherwise specified Scars manifest during stressful moments of the Vigil.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Calling Card (pg.330 HtV)</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Beat: The character leaves behind a consistent clue. The character delays ending action on the Vigil to leave an unremarkable symbol. The character leaves the calling card while in non-hunt activities.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Denial (pg.330)</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">He suffers a -3 to Tactics or Endowments that rely on the acceptance of the supernatural.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Beat: The character explains away or destroys obvious evidence of the supernatural. The character takes an action under the erroneous belief that the supernatural does not exist. The character fails a Tactic or Endowment roll.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Hypochondria (331)</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">He suffers a -2 penalty to all Physical rolls whenever he feels 'exposed' to disease.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Beat: Fails a roll due to the Condition. Refuses to enter an area that is 'contaminated'. Takes action to try to 'cure' or 'protect' himself from phantom illness that interferes with the Vigil.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Overkill (331)</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Beat: Character mutilates the body of a monster. Takes a whole scene to ensure &quot;all traces&quot; have been wiped clean. Fails to regain Willpower until the body is destroyed to his liking. Puts himself or allies in danger to destroy the body.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Sadism (332)</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Beat: Needlessly physically or emotionally torments her quarry. Endangers himself or another to deliver more pain to her victim. Refuses orders to kill or drive off a victim in order to inflict more harm.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Sexual Deviance (333)</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Beat: Engages in sexual contact with the target of the quarry (or a fellow member of the cell). Fails a social roll due to this condition. Endangers himself or another to engage in some sexual act in the midst of the hunt.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Killers Aura</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Those who have been on the hunt for some time have an indelible mark on their psyche. This expresses itself in a thousand-yard stare, grisly trophies from victims, gruesome gallows humor, or a barely restrained violence.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Suffers a -2 to First Impression rolls.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Beat: Character uses hard leverage when soft leverage would have sufficed. Fails a first impression roll due to this condition. Takes a &quot;trophy&quot; from a hunt.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Delusional</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">The character believes in an utterly irrational explanation for the supernatural. Regardless of the nature of this belief, religion, conspiracy theory, psuedoscientific, this Scar is usually garnished by a breaking point where events undermine the character's belief.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Beat: The character takes a detrimental action based on some &quot;fact&quot; of their delusion. Character wastes a scene rationalizing something that doesn't fit into their belief system.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Recklessness</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Some hunters deal with their numerous brushes with death by skewing their perception of what constitutes a threat. A hunter with such a psychological Scar is almost incapable of reacting appropriately when faced with anything other than obvious direct danger.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Beat: Character refuse to take preparations before a dangerous endeavor. The character fails to warn or help others against danger. The character doesn't take seriously some mundane threat.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Fascination</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Hunters when dedicated to uncovering the secrets of the World of Darkness become fixated upon a particular subject focusing on this pursuit to the exclusion of all other activities.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Beat: The character compromises a plan, another hunter, or an innocent to garnish a piece of information. The character makes a compromising deal with a monster in exchange for a lead.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Xenophobia</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">The character has a decisively black and white world view. Namely 'us vs them', they distrust those anyone not in their trusted circle and are exclusionary even to their deficit.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Beat: The character complicates a situation by refusing to cooperate or assumes (incorrectly) the worst of an 'outsider'. The character sacrifices an outsiders safety to secure some benefit.</span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1783-spanishinquisition","name":"SpanishInquisition","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1783_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"60"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}